1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,106
Two vials.

2
00:00:01,106 --> 00:00:02,851
Both look perfectly clear.

3
00:00:02,851 --> 00:00:03,984
Are they the same?

4
00:00:03,984 --> 00:00:05,585
Your eyes cannot tell you.

5
00:00:05,585 --> 00:00:10,468
Cloudiness, flakes, or particles you can see are a real warning sign.

6
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:16,145
Pharmacopeia standards treat visible particles as a reason to reject a vial.

7
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:18,463
But clear does not mean unchanged.

8
00:00:18,463 --> 00:00:22,994
Much of what happens to the amino-chain, the peptide, is invisible.

9
00:00:22,994 --> 00:00:27,903
Chemical changes like oxidation leave the liquid looking exactly the same.

10
00:00:27,903 --> 00:00:30,364
And some clumps are far too small to see.

11
00:00:30,364 --> 00:00:32,434
Labs need instruments to count them.

12
00:00:32,434 --> 00:00:35,338
So a clear vial is not a clean bill of health.

13
00:00:35,338 --> 00:00:36,666
Cloudy is a warning.

14
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:37,968
Clear proves nothing.

15
00:00:37,968 --> 00:00:41,692
The label, the storage, and the time are what you can trust.

16
00:00:41,692 --> 00:00:42,968
Day eight of ten.

17
00:00:42,968 --> 00:00:44,934
Follow so you don't miss day nine.

